==================================================
SQL INTERVIEW GUIDE (1-20)
==================================================

Sample Tables

Employee

EmpId | Name  | DeptId | Salary
------+-------+--------+--------
1     | John  | 10     | 50000
2     | Mary  | 10     | 70000
3     | Steve | 20     | 60000
4     | Tom   | 20     | 80000
5     | David | 30     | 90000

Department

DeptId | DeptName
-------+----------
10     | IT
20     | HR
30     | Finance

==================================================
1. Find All Employees
==================================================

Query

SELECT *
FROM Employee;

Output

1 John  10 50000
2 Mary  10 70000
3 Steve 20 60000
4 Tom   20 80000
5 David 30 90000

Explanation

SELECT * returns all columns.
No filtering is applied.

==================================================
2. Find Employees With Salary > 60000
==================================================

Query

SELECT Name, Salary
FROM Employee
WHERE Salary > 60000;

Output

Mary  70000
Tom   80000
David 90000

Explanation

WHERE filters rows before returning them.

==================================================
3. Find Distinct Departments
==================================================

Query

SELECT DISTINCT DeptId
FROM Employee;

Output

10
20
30

Explanation

DISTINCT removes duplicate values.

==================================================
4. Count Employees Per Department
==================================================

Query

SELECT DeptId,
       COUNT(*) AS EmpCount
FROM Employee
GROUP BY DeptId;

Output

10  2
20  2
30  1

Explanation

GROUP BY creates groups.
COUNT(*) counts records in each group.

==================================================
5. Departments Having More Than 1 Employee
==================================================

Query

SELECT DeptId,
       COUNT(*) AS EmpCount
FROM Employee
GROUP BY DeptId
HAVING COUNT(*) > 1;

Output

10  2
20  2

Explanation

HAVING filters groups after aggregation.

==================================================
6. INNER JOIN Example
==================================================

Query

SELECT e.Name,
       d.DeptName
FROM Employee e
INNER JOIN Department d
ON e.DeptId = d.DeptId;

Output

John   IT
Mary   IT
Steve  HR
Tom    HR
David  Finance

Explanation

INNER JOIN returns only matching rows.

==================================================
7. LEFT JOIN Example
==================================================

Query

SELECT e.Name,
       d.DeptName
FROM Employee e
LEFT JOIN Department d
ON e.DeptId = d.DeptId;

Output

John   IT
Mary   IT
Steve  HR
Tom    HR
David  Finance

Explanation

LEFT JOIN returns all rows from Employee.

Example

Employee : Alex DeptId=40

Result

Alex NULL

==================================================
8. Average Salary Per Department
==================================================

Query

SELECT DeptId,
       AVG(Salary) AS AvgSalary
FROM Employee
GROUP BY DeptId;

Output

10  60000
20  70000
30  90000

Explanation

Dept 10

(50000 + 70000)/2
= 60000

Dept 20

(60000 + 80000)/2
= 70000

Dept 30

90000

==================================================
9. Employees Above Average Salary
==================================================

Company Average

(50000+70000+60000+80000+90000)
/5

=70000

Query

SELECT Name
FROM Employee
WHERE Salary >
(
   SELECT AVG(Salary)
   FROM Employee
);

Output

Tom
David

Explanation

Subquery calculates average salary.
Employees above average are returned.

==================================================
10. Highest Salary
==================================================

Query

SELECT MAX(Salary)
FROM Employee;

Output

90000

Explanation

MAX() returns the highest value.

==================================================
11. Second Highest Salary
==================================================

Query

SELECT MAX(Salary)
FROM Employee
WHERE Salary <
(
   SELECT MAX(Salary)
   FROM Employee
);

Output

80000

Explanation

Ignore highest salary (90000).
Find highest among remaining rows.

==================================================
12. Third Highest Salary Using ROW_NUMBER
==================================================

Query

WITH X AS
(
   SELECT Salary,
          ROW_NUMBER()
          OVER(ORDER BY Salary DESC) rn
   FROM Employee
)
SELECT Salary
FROM X
WHERE rn = 3;

Output

70000

Explanation

90000 -> 1
80000 -> 2
70000 -> 3

Third highest salary = 70000

==================================================
13. ROW_NUMBER Example
==================================================

Query

SELECT Name,
       Salary,
       ROW_NUMBER()
       OVER(ORDER BY Salary DESC) rn
FROM Employee;

Output

David 90000 1
Tom   80000 2
Mary  70000 3
Steve 60000 4
John  50000 5

Explanation

ROW_NUMBER gives unique numbers.
No duplicates are allowed.

==================================================
14. RANK Example
==================================================

Query

SELECT Name,
       Salary,
       RANK()
       OVER(ORDER BY Salary DESC) rnk
FROM Employee;

Output

David 90000 1
Tom   80000 2
Mary  70000 3
Steve 60000 4
John  50000 5

Explanation

RANK assigns same rank to ties.

Example

David 90000 1
Tom   80000 2
Alex  80000 2
Mary  70000 4

Rank 3 is skipped.

==================================================
15. Running Total
==================================================

Query

SELECT Name,
       Salary,
       SUM(Salary)
       OVER(ORDER BY Salary) AS RunningTotal
FROM Employee;

Output

John  50000  50000
Steve 60000 110000
Mary  70000 180000
Tom   80000 260000
David 90000 350000

Explanation

Running sum is maintained row by row.

==================================================
16. Department Average Using PARTITION BY
==================================================

Query

SELECT Name,
       Salary,
       AVG(Salary)
       OVER(PARTITION BY DeptId) AS DeptAvg
FROM Employee;

Output

John   50000 60000
Mary   70000 60000
Steve  60000 70000
Tom    80000 70000
David  90000 90000

Explanation

Dept 10 Avg = 60000
Dept 20 Avg = 70000
Dept 30 Avg = 90000

PARTITION BY creates groups without reducing rows.

==================================================
17. Find Duplicate Records
==================================================

Customer

1 a@gmail.com
2 b@gmail.com
3 a@gmail.com
4 c@gmail.com

Query

SELECT Email,
       COUNT(*)
FROM Customer
GROUP BY Email
HAVING COUNT(*) > 1;

Output

a@gmail.com 2

Explanation

GROUP BY forms email groups.
HAVING finds duplicate emails.

==================================================
18. UNION
==================================================

Employee Names

John
Mary
Tom

Customer Names

Mary
David
Tom

Query

SELECT Name FROM Employee
UNION
SELECT Name FROM Customer;

Output

John
Mary
Tom
David

Explanation

UNION removes duplicates.

==================================================
19. UNION ALL
==================================================

Query

SELECT Name FROM Employee
UNION ALL
SELECT Name FROM Customer;

Output

John
Mary
Tom
Mary
David
Tom

Explanation

UNION ALL keeps duplicates.
Faster than UNION.

==================================================
20. Top 3 Highest Salaries
==================================================

Query

SELECT Salary
FROM Employee
ORDER BY Salary DESC
FETCH FIRST 3 ROWS ONLY;

Output

90000
80000
70000

Explanation

ORDER BY sorts salaries descending.
FETCH FIRST 3 ROWS ONLY returns top 3 rows.

==================================================
30-SECOND SQL REVISION
==================================================

1. SELECT *            -> All Rows
2. WHERE               -> Filter Rows
3. DISTINCT            -> Remove Duplicates
4. GROUP BY            -> Create Groups
5. HAVING              -> Filter Groups
6. INNER JOIN          -> Matching Rows
7. LEFT JOIN           -> All Left Rows
8. AVG()               -> Average
9. Subquery            -> Query Inside Query
10. MAX()              -> Highest Value
11. Second Highest     -> MAX + Subquery
12. Nth Highest        -> ROW_NUMBER
13. ROW_NUMBER         -> Unique Numbering
14. RANK               -> Ranking With Gaps
15. Running Total      -> SUM() OVER()
16. PARTITION BY       -> Group Metrics
17. Duplicate Records  -> GROUP BY + HAVING
18. UNION              -> Remove Duplicates
19. UNION ALL          -> Keep Duplicates
20. Top N Records      -> ORDER BY + FETCH/LIMIT

